# Generative page prompt — Meeting Orchestrator (v5 parity spike)

Paste into **Add page → Describe a page** in the model-driven app designer. Run it in three passes: Pass 1 (base page), then Pass 2 and Pass 3 as refinement prompts. Trying to get all of it in one prompt tends to lose the detail.

---

## PASS 1 — the page

Build a page called **Book a client meeting** for private-bank relationship managers. It replaces a six-step manual booking process with three steps on one page. The user must never scroll back up to fix something, and never leave this page to finish the job.

**Data (Dataverse):** Meeting, Meeting Type (option set, searchable — there are ~40), Contact (clients), Account, Associated Party, User (colleague directory), Building, Room, Catering Order, AI Extraction, Audit Event.

**Layout:** a single scrolling page, max width about 1100px, with a persistent action bar pinned to the bottom of the viewport. Three numbered stages shown as a slim progress strip at the top: 1 Details, 2 Time, 3 Confirm. Selecting a stage scrolls to it; stages 2 and 3 stay disabled until the stage before is valid.

### Stage 1 — Details

Show only three fields by default:

- **Customer** — type-ahead search over Contacts in the current user's own book. Results show name, segment and last-meeting date. Below the results, a quieter link "Search all clients" which, when used, shows an amber inline notice that the search will be logged to the access audit, and requires a reason to be picked before results appear.
- **Meeting type** — a searchable dropdown over the Meeting Type option set. Show the option-set name in small monospace text at the bottom of the dropdown so the user knows where the values come from.
- **When** — three choices: This week, Next week, Pick a date range.

Under those, a single text button **"Change the details"** which expands to reveal: Duration, Location, Building, Format (In person / Video call), and Catering. Collapsed by default. If any revealed field has been changed from its default, show a small count next to the button.

Also in stage 1:

- An **email drop zone** at the top: "Drop the client's email here" accepting .eml and .txt. While processing, show a slim progress line, not a blocking spinner — the rest of the page stays usable and fields fill in as they resolve.
- **People** — attendee chips with avatars and initials. An "Add people" button opens a searchable modal over the colleague directory. An "Associated parties" button opens a second modal listing the selected client's associated parties from Dataverse.

### Stage 2 — Time

A ranked list of three to five suggested slots, each as a selectable card showing day, date, time, room name and building, and a one-line reason ("All five attendees free; boardroom available"). Selected cards are visibly chosen; more than one can be selected to send the client options.

Below the list, three actions: **Widen the window**, **Make the guest list smaller**, **Change the details**.

### Stage 3 — Confirm

A read-only summary of everything, grouped: Client, Meeting, People, Room, Catering. Each group has an inline Edit link that scrolls back to the relevant field.

### The action bar (pinned, always visible)

Left: a one-line summary of current state ("Hana Voss · Portfolio review · Thu 31 Jul, 10:00"). Right: the primary button, whose label changes by stage — **Confirm details & find times** → **Choose these times** → **Send to client**. When required fields are missing, the button is disabled and the bar states plainly what is missing ("Add a customer and a meeting type").

**Visual style:** match the model-driven app shell using Fluent UI 9. Restrained and professional — private banking, not consumer. White cards on a warm off-white background, one dark green accent for primary actions, one muted gold reserved for AI and warnings. No gradients, no emoji, no decorative icons. Body text 13–14px, generous line height, comfortable spacing.

